.TH "MAW-THEMES" "7" "" "maw 0.2.3" "maw manual"
.SH NAME
maw-themes \- colors from your wallpaper
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
maw can make a color palette from your wallpaper with matugen (https://github.com/InioX/matugen), for every module to use. It's optional: nothing about it runs, and matugen isn't needed, until you set \fBmaw.theme\fR in \fBconfig.nix\fR or run \fBmaw wallpaper\fR. Wallpapers live in \fBstatic/wallpapers/\fR, so every machine has them:
.PP
.EX
maw wallpaper ~/Pictures/forest.jpg   # copied into static/wallpapers/, then themed
maw wallpaper random                  # another wallpaper from static/wallpapers/
maw wallpaper                         # which one it is now
.EE
.PP
.EX
copy static/wallpapers/forest.jpg
theme static/wallpapers/forest.jpg: color 2 of 4, dark #a6d0b0
eval niri waybar fuzzel
update ~/.config/waybar/style.css
reload waybar
.EE
.PP
An image has a few candidate colors a palette can grow from, most dominant first; each \fBmaw wallpaper\fR picks one at random, so the same wallpaper can come back in a different color. Then maw activates, so modules that use the theme rebuild and their programs reload. Changing the wallpaper isn't a generation, and leaves nothing to commit. A file that uses the theme is rendered twice in the same evaluation: once with the real palette, placed from \fB~/.local/state/maw/themed/<machine>/\fR, and once with every color a placeholder gray (\fB#808080\fR) and the wallpaper as \fB<wallpaper>\fR, which is the copy \fBout/\fR and git keep. A new wallpaper changes only the first, so \fBgit diff\fR shows only real config changes. \fBmaw build\fR lists themed files as \fBwrite themed/<file>\fR. A machine activating with \fB--no-build\fR before its first build places the placeholder copy until then.
.PP
Because the placeholder render has the same palette keys with gray values, a module can read \fBtheme.colors.primary\fR or \fBtheme.wallpaper\fR directly; \fBtheme\fR is only \fB{ }\fR before the first wallpaper.
.PP
In \fBconfig.nix\fR, the palette's settings, with their defaults (setting \fBmaw.theme\fR also themes a fresh machine from the first wallpaper):
.PP
.EX
maw.theme = {
  mode = "dark";                  # or "light"
  scheme = "scheme-tonal-spot";   # or scheme-vibrant, scheme-expressive, scheme-fidelity, scheme-content, ...
  contrast = 0;                   # -1 to 1
};
.EE
.PP
Modules and \fBconfig.nix\fR read it as \fBtheme\fR (see \fBmaw help modules\fR):
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtheme.colors\fR: Material You colors as \fB#rrggbb\fR: \fBprimary\fR, \fBon_primary\fR, \fBsurface\fR, \fBon_surface\fR, \fBsurface_container\fR, \fBoutline\fR, \fBerror\fR, \fBsecondary\fR, \fBtertiary\fR, and the rest of the scheme
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtheme.base16\fR: \fBbase00\fR through \fBbase0F\fR, for programs with base16 themes
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtheme.wallpaper\fR: the image's absolute path
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtheme.mode\fR, \fBtheme.source\fR: the mode and the color the palette grew from
.PP
To show the wallpaper, declare it like anything else. A service is best: the path is in its run file, so changing the wallpaper restarts it (your compositor has to share its display with user services; see \fBlib.service\fR in \fBmaw help modules\fR):
.PP
.EX
{ lib, theme, ... }:
lib.service "swaybg" {
  scope = "user";
  run = "exec swaybg -i ${theme.wallpaper} -m fill";
}
.EE
.PP
To change it on a timer, run \fBmaw wallpaper random\fR from anything that runs on a schedule, like a user service that sleeps between changes. Theming needs \fBmatugen\fR (\fBmaw install matugen\fR); maw itself doesn't depend on it.
.PP
